# Optimizing Physics Engines & Creative Coding in the Browser

When building interactive simulations in the browser, you quickly hit a wall: the JavaScript engine is fast, but physics math is *expensive*. Whether you're simulating thousands of electrostatic particles or building a multi-jointed physics tank, naive implementations will tank your framerate.

In this post, I'll break down two major technical hurdles I tackled in my recent simulations: optimizing an N-Body particle system using **Spatial Hashing**, and building a complex, multi-jointed rigid body tank using **Planck.js** and **p5.js**.

---

## 1. Escaping the <code>O(N<sup>2</sup>)</code> Trap: Spatial Hashing

In my **Charges** simulation, I needed to calculate the Coulomb force between hundreds of interacting protons and electrons. 

### The Problem
The naive approach requires checking every particle against every other particle. For <code>N</code> particles, this results in <code>N × (N - 1)</code> calculations per frame. If you have 1,000 particles, that's nearly 1 million force calculations every single frame. Your browser will choke, and your FPS will drop to single digits.

### The Solution: Spatial Hashing
Instead of checking the entire grid, we divide the canvas into a grid of cells (e.g., <code>CELL_SIZE = 100px</code>). Each particle is assigned to a cell based on its coordinates. Because electrostatic forces drop off rapidly over distance, a particle only needs to interact with other particles in its **own cell and the 8 immediately adjacent cells**.

Here is how I implemented the cell indexing and neighbor lookup:

```javascript
// 1. Calculate the cell index for the particle
this.cX = ~~(this.x / CELL_SIZE);
this.cY = ~~(this.y / CELL_SIZE);
this.cell = this.cX + " " + this.cY;

// 2. Pre-calculate the 9 neighboring cell keys
this.selfAndNeighborCellsName = [
  this.cell,
  this.cX + 1 + " " + (this.cY - 1),
  this.cX - 1 + " " + (this.cY - 1),
  this.cX     + " " + (this.cY - 1),
  this.cX + 1 + " " + this.cY,
  this.cX - 1 + " " + this.cY,
  this.cX + 1 + " " + (this.cY + 1),
  this.cX - 1 + " " + (this.cY + 1),
  this.cX     + " " + (this.cY + 1)
];
```

When applying forces, we simply iterate through this 9-cell array and check the <code>COLLISION_GRID</code> dictionary:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < this.selfAndNeighborCellsName.length; i++) {
  let particlesInCell = COLLISION_GRID[this.selfAndNeighborCellsName[i]];
  if (!particlesInCell) continue;
  
  for (let particle of particlesInCell) {
    // Calculate and apply Coulomb force...
  }
}
```

**The Result:** The time complexity drops from <code>O(N<sup>2</sup>)</code> to near <code>O(N)</code>. The simulation smoothly handles thousands of particles at 60 FPS without breaking a sweat.

---

## 2. Complex Rigid Body Dynamics with Planck.js

While particle systems are about *optimizing calculations*, rigid body simulations are about *accurate physical constraints*. For my **Tank Simulator**, I used `p5.play` (which wraps the robust `planck.js` physics engine) to build a fully functional, multi-jointed vehicle.

### The Joint System
A tank isn't just a single box. It's a chassis, a rotating turret, and a track system. I achieved this by chaining different physics joints:

1. **<code>WheelJoint</code>**: Used for the independent suspension. I tuned the <code>springiness</code> and <code>damping</code> to give the wheels realistic bounce over the terrain.
2. **<code>DistanceJoint</code>**: Used between adjacent wheels to simulate track tension and the conveyer effect, keeping the wheels synchronized.
3. **<code>HingeJoint</code>**: Used to attach the turret to the chassis, allowing it to rotate independently of the tank's movement.

### Dynamic Torque & Gear Ratios
To make the tank feel heavy and mechanical, I implemented a manual transmission system with an array of gear ratios:

```javascript
gears: [
  { number: "N", ratio: 0 }, 
  { number: "I", ratio: 0.125 }, 
  { number: "II", ratio: 0.25 }, 
  // ... up to "VII", ratio: 2
]
```

When the player shifts gears, the engine's <code>ratio</code> updates. Crucially, the maximum power applied to the wheel axles is dynamically scaled inversely to the gear ratio:

```javascript
switchGear: (direction) => {
  this.base.engine.gear = constrain(this.base.engine.gear + direction, 0, this.gears.length - 1);
  this.base.engine.ratio = this.gears[this.base.engine.gear].ratio;
  
  // Lower gears = more torque (maxPower)
  this.axels.forEach(axel => {
    axel.maxPower = 0.3 / this.base.engine.ratio; 
  });
}
```

### Newton's Third Law: Physical Recoil
When the tank fires, the projectile gets an <code>applyForce()</code> in the direction of the barrel. To simulate realistic recoil, I applied an equal and opposite force to the turret itself:

```javascript
// Offset the bearing by Math.PI to push backwards
this.turret.bearing = this.turret.rotation + Math.PI;
this.turret.applyForce(this.turret.projectileSpeed);
```

Because the turret is attached to the chassis via a <code>HingeJoint</code>, this backward force physically pushes the entire tank backward, requiring the player to use the engine to maintain their position.

---

## The Takeaway

Creative coding in the browser requires a dual mindset. You need **algorithmic optimization** (like Spatial Hashing) to handle massive datasets efficiently, and **physics constraints** (like Joints and Torque scaling) to make those datasets behave like the real world. 

By understanding the underlying math and leveraging the right tools, you can push the limits of what's possible in a standard web browser.

